 {
  "ghsa_id": "GHSA-7hp8-65ch-5whp",
  "cve_id": "CVE-2026-87902",
  "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/WordPress/wordpress-develop/security-advisories/GHSA-7hp8-65ch-5whp",
  "html_url": "https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/security/advisories/GHSA-7hp8-65ch-5whp",
  "summary": "Unauthenticated path traversal in page-template resolution leading to conditional RCE",
  "description": "An unauthenticated attacker can make `get_page_template()` page-template resolution include a chosen readable local `.php` file outside the active theme directories. If relevant pre-conditions for both the server environment and the active theme are met, this can lead to RCE.\r\n\r\nThe pre-conditions are:\r\n\r\n* The active child or parent theme contains a top-level directory whose name starts with `page-` (e.g. `page-templates`). This affects the legacy Twenty Twelve and Twenty Fourteen themes, as well as some popular third party themes such as Neve, Hestia, and Sydney.\r\n* A chosen local `.php` target file exists on the server and is readable by the web server account. The well known `pearcmd.php` PEAR→RCE transition can be used for this when `register_argc_argv` is set to `On`. The official `php` image for Docker is affected, and the default cPanel configuration is affected when PHP prior to 8.5 is in use.\r\n\r\nWordPress 7.1.2 has been released containing a fix for the vulnerability, and as a courtesy to users on older branches the fix has been backported to all branches back to 4.7.\r\n\r\nDiscovered and responsibly disclosed by [Robert Ressl](https://ressl.ch/).",
  "severity": "critical",
  "identifiers": [
   {
    "value": "GHSA-7hp8-65ch-5whp",
